FactionBackstory (
    faction_id: "frontier",
    canonical_name: "The Frontier Corps",
    motto: "Strike. Breach. Vanish.",
    summary: "Outer-system irregulars descended from the Mars rebellion of 2168. Frontier doctrine prizes mobility, sabotage, and overwhelming local force. Rust orange and dark canvas; leather reinforcement; ranks earned by results, not by paperwork.",
    history: "When the Olympus Mons treaty collapsed in 2169, the Mars Free State did not surrender. It splintered. Some cells fled to the Belt; some went to ground inside Olympus itself, in caverns the old terraforming crews had drilled and abandoned. The rest formed the first Frontier Corps, a confederation of demolition crews, drill teams, and orbital pirates who agreed on nothing except that they would never wear blue again. Their first action was the burning of the Concordat customs office on Phobos. Their second was a six-day standoff at the Olympus Mons rim. Their third was a deal with the Belt that nobody in the Coalition has ever been able to break.\n\nFor a hundred years the Frontier was less a faction than a contagion. Wherever the Coalition fortified, the Frontier tunneled under. Wherever a corporate fleet landed to harvest, the Frontier blew the lift cables and walked away with the cargo. They built no cities, kept no capital, and signed no charters. They issued nicknames instead of ranks. They paid in salvage instead of cash. They lost more cells than they kept, and the survivors kept getting better. By 2230, the Frontier was the second-largest single armed force in the Sol system, and the only one with no public muster roll.\n\nThe modern Frontier is leaner. The Pirate Surge of 2229 stripped out the worst of the warlord cells; the Mars Concord of 2241 absorbed the rest. What is left are working crews — sabotage specialists, demolition leads, scout drivers, drill operators, and the kind of medic who learned trauma surgery in a tunnel by lamp light. They take contracts. They take grudges. They take Coalition gear and modify it, often badly, and sell it back to the Coalition's own supply depots through three layers of front companies. They are not loved. They are not respected. They are, in the words of a Coalition white paper that leaked in 2241, 'the most cost-effective army Mars has ever produced.'",
    philosophy: "Strike fast. Breach the wall, the door, the contract, the trust. Vanish before the response arrives. The Frontier believes that any fortification is a target and any target is a contract and any contract is a paycheck. Loyalty is to the crew, the crew is to the score, and the score belongs to whoever crossed the wire first. Officers earn rank by completing jobs nobody else would take; they lose rank by getting caught.",
    relationships: [
        FactionRelation (other: "coalition", stance: "uneasy_truce", notes: "The Olympus line is observed. The supply lanes are not. Frontier raids on Coalition convoys averaged 1.4 per week in 2241, by Coalition estimate; the Frontier estimate is higher and prouder."),
        FactionRelation (other: "ronin", stance: "transactional_respect", notes: "Frontier crews hire Ronin contracts for sieges they cannot finish themselves. The arrangement works because Ronin do not haggle and Frontier do not betray. Neither side claims to like it."),
        FactionRelation (other: "synth", stance: "hostile_distrust", notes: "Synth nodes predicted six Frontier ambushes in 2240 and informed the Coalition before they happened. The Frontier has not forgotten. The Synth nodes have, because that data is no longer load-bearing in their model."),
        FactionRelation (other: "collective", stance: "neutral_curiosity", notes: "Crystalfold settlements are too poor to rob and too strange to fight. Frontier crews trade ore for crystal lensing and call it even."),
        FactionRelation (other: "husks", stance: "active_hostility", notes: "The Husks raid the Frontier's underground refits. The Frontier responds with thermite. The casualty figures are kept on a chalkboard in a tunnel outside Olympus and updated weekly."),
        FactionRelation (other: "collegium", stance: "commercial_partnership", notes: "Frontier crews supply Collegium expeditions with terrain knowledge and demolition labor. The Collegium pays in experimental gear that occasionally works. The Frontier finds the failure cases acceptable; the Collegium finds the survival rates acceptable."),
        FactionRelation (other: "starlight", stance: "covert_rivalry", notes: "Starlight maps anomalies. The Frontier follows the maps and gets there first. Starlight does not press the issue, because the Frontier does the part of the work Starlight charters explicitly forbid."),
    ],
    territory: "Mars underground network (Olympus to Pavonis tunnel ring); Phobos crater habitats; eight Belt asteroids registered under no flag; an itinerant orbital platform that changes its transponder ID every Mars-month.",
    aesthetic: "Rust orange and dark green field gear with leather and canvas reinforcement. Steel-toed boots. Welded patchwork helmets, no two alike, often with a single dented eye lens and a respirator that has been re-fitted from older gear. Tools and weapons are carried on improvised webbing. Crews tattoo their nicknames on their forearms; the official faction insignia is a stylized drill bit crossed by a wrench. Banners are spray-painted onto whatever surface is convenient.",
    voice_characteristics: "Rough cadence. Drawling vowels. First names only, often shortened to one syllable. Heavy use of trade jargon — 'crack the seal', 'walk the wire', 'eat the contract'. Frontier NPCs make jokes during firefights and curse during reloads. Officers ('leads') give orders as suggestions; the suggestions are not optional."
)
